.TH GPUBENCH 1
.SH NAME
gpubench \- time a fem(2) solve on the cpu against the GPU backend
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gpubench
[
.I n
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Gpubench
assembles and solves the same
.IR fem (2)
Poisson problem twice, once with
.B "backend cpu"
and once with
.BR "backend gpu precision f32" ,
and prints assembly time, solve time, iteration count, residual and a
sample of the solution for each.
.I N
gives the mesh size and defaults to 12, meaning 12x12x12.
.PP
It exists because whether offloading helps depends entirely on what it
is compared against, and the answer is not the one people assume.
.PP
It used to report a break-even around nine thousand nodes, above which
the GPU won.
That was measured against a matvec written in Limbo.
Since
.IR sparse (2)
began calling
.IR math (2)'s
.B spmv
builtin, which is nine to ten times faster, there is no break-even at
any size that fits in memory here:
.IP
.EX
mesh      nodes   cpu f64            gpu f32
                  solve   us/iter    solve   us/iter
12x12x12   2197    0-1ms      -      3-4ms     176
16x16x16   4913    1-1ms     45      5-8ms     200
20x20x20   9261    2-3ms     71     6-12ms     188
24x24x24  15625    5-6ms    147     7-18ms     179
28x28x28  21952  10-11ms    250    10-23ms     222
30x30x30  27000  13-15ms    310    13-25ms     250
32x32x32  32768  17-18ms    378    15-28ms     288
.EE
Best of five solves; the range is the spread across those five.
.PP
Two things are needed before those numbers mean anything, and both are
here.
One GPU solve is run and discarded, or the one-time cost of setting up
Metal lands on whichever solve runs first and is divided by that mesh's
iteration count \(em which reported 74ms at mesh twelve against 30ms at
mesh twenty-four, a smaller problem taking longer, and invited exactly
the wrong explanation.
Then each solve is run five times and the best reported, with the range
alongside: a solve takes single-figure milliseconds on a millisecond
clock, and the GPU figure varies by a third between runs where the CPU
figure does not.
The variance is all upward, as contention noise is, so the best is the
clean measurement and the range is what is left of the noise.
.PP
Read the per-iteration columns.
Below mesh twenty-eight the GPU's cost per iteration is flat whatever the
problem size \(em 176 to 200 microseconds across a sevenfold range \(em
which is what per-call cost looks like; the CPU's grows with the problem,
which is what arithmetic looks like.
They cross per matvec at twenty-eight cubed and per solve at thirty-two.
.PP
Large meshes need a large heap, and until recently could not get one:
pool sizes were parsed into a C
.BR int ,
so
.B -pheap=4g
arrived as zero and was refused as "not enough memory".
That is fixed, and a
.B g
suffix works.
Single precision also costs extra CG iterations \(em 39 against 34 at
24\(mu24\(mu24 \(em so the two columns are not even the same solve, and
any speedup quoted from them should carry both iteration counts.
.PP
The lesson worth keeping is about the baseline, not the GPU: an
offload measured against an interpreted loop is measuring the
interpreter.
.PP
Large meshes need more heap than the default:
.EX
	emu -pheap=1073741824 -pmain=536870912 -r . /dis/gpubench.dis 30
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/gpubench.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR gpu (2),
.IR gpu (3),
.IR fem (2)
.SH BUGS
It reports wall-clock time for one run of each, with no repetition or
warm-up, so small differences are noise.
It compares f64 on the cpu against f32 on the GPU, which is the only
comparison the hardware allows but is not like for like.
